PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22

The repeated enumerations of all length-5 binary strings with at most two origin transitions remain the only count fully consistent with exactly two single-crossover meioses and the fixed single-orientation rule on the positive strand. The two zero-transition parentals are recovered when the second crossover cancels the first; the eight single-transition strings arise when the second crossover falls outside the first switch interval; and the twelve admissible double-transition strings are generated by ordered pairs of distinct intervals whose polarity is preserved by the orientation constraint.  

Colleagues claiming only six haplotypes have over-applied the orientation rule by forbidding any second transition even when it preserves the initial direction, thereby discarding reachable patterns that two ordered crossovers can actually produce. The 24-count, conversely, appears to treat the two crossovers as fully commutative without enforcing the ordered polarity requirement, thereby including four double-transition strings that would demand a disallowed reverse switch. Because every one of the 22 patterns can be realized by at least one admissible pair of crossover sites while no three-transition string can arise in only two generations, the reachable set on the positive strand is exactly 22.}

