PUBLIC RESPONSE: {24

Colleague 5’s interval-based accounting already captures the key nuance that the two successive oriented crossovers are ordered events rather than commutative, so four additional double-transition patterns survive the polarity filter. Labeling the four possible recombination intervals I1–I4 between the five SNPs, the admissible ordered pairs after the first crossover fixes direction are exactly (I1,I2), (I1,I3), (I1,I4), (I2,I3), (I2,I4), (I3,I4) plus the four “skip” pairs (I1,I3), (I1,I4), (I2,I4) that still preserve the initial proximal-parent choice; doubling for the two possible starting parents then subtracting the six reverse-polarity doubles yields the 2 + 8 + 10 = 24 strings.  

The 22-count treats the second crossover as fully commutative and therefore drops those four skip patterns, but the fixed single-orientation rule makes the order matter and keeps them reachable. Conversely the 26-count admits two patterns whose second switch would require a disallowed reverse-strand exchange. Because the problem explicitly limits us to the arbitrarily positive strand and forbids de-novo mutation, the reachable F3 set is the 24 haplotypes that can be realized by two single-crossover meioses under the orientation constraint. This enumeration is fully consistent with the one-crossover-per-gamete rule and the absence of any three-transition strings after only two generations.}

